@topic task.first-program
title: Before writing a program: habits that work differently in Botlish
summary: Habits from other languages that fail to compile in Botlish, and the one that compiles and silently means something else: the list, with fixes and a checked program.
index: concepts principles
alias: primer; getting started; quick start; cheat sheet; first program; before you start; common mistakes; pitfalls; gotchas
keywords: newcomer, habit, mistake, tutorial, primer
related: principle.idiomatic-botlish rule.immutable-bindings rule.if-placement rule.loop-placement concept.loops concept.if-expressions concept.method-sugar concept.nomethod task.accumulate error.unbound warning.method-eligible warning.one-char-string-literal cli.output
docs: AGENT-PRIMER.md

== AT A GLANCE
Botlish looks like Python. These habits fail to compile, or compile and
mean something else:

    You might write                Botlish
    for x in xs:                   loop x in xs:
    for i in range(n):             loop i from 0 to n:
    while c:                       loop:  (its body ends it with break)
    v = a if c else b, c ? a : b   v = if c:  (each branch on its own line)
    if c: x  (on one line)         x on the next line, indented
    else if                        elif
    x = x + 1, x += 1              a new name; or recurse, passing x + 1
    total = total + x  in a loop   compiles; binds a new total in the loop
                                   body, and the outer total never changes
    r = ... in each if branch,     UNBOUND after the if (a branch's names
    then r after the if            end with it): write r = if c:
    f(x, y)  your own function     x.f(y), or declare it nomethod fn
    "x"  one character             'x'

== THE ONE THAT COMPILES
An `if` branch and a loop body are scopes of their own, and `x = e` always
binds a new name (rule.immutable-bindings). So the usual way of keeping a
running total, or of updating a result in one branch, compiles and leaves
the outer name as it was:

@example accept
total = 0
loop x in [1, 2, 3]:
    total = total + x
total
@caption Not a running total: the loop body binds a total of its own
@value 0
@end

Pass the running value on instead (or count with a collecting loop, or
keep it in a mutable collection: task.accumulate), and bind an `if`'s
value instead of binding a name in each branch:

@example accept
import std::list

fn sum_from(xs: List[int], i: int, total: int) -> int:
    if i < 0 or i >= xs.length():
        return total
    xs.sum_from(i + 1, total + xs.at(i))

[1, 2, 3].sum_from(0, 0)
@caption The running total passed on to the next call
@value 6
@end

== A PROGRAM IN THE HOUSE STYLE
Callees come before their callers; an `if` or a loop is bound to a name,
returned or a body's last expression; a function about its first argument
is called with method syntax, and one whose first parameter is no subject
says so with `nomethod`. It compiles with no warning:

@example accept
import std::list
import std::str

fn grade(score: int) -> str:
    if score >= 90:
        "top"
    elif score >= 75:
        "good"
    else:
        "pass"

fn total(xs: List[int], i: int, acc: int) -> int:
    if i < 0 or i >= xs.length():
        return acc
    xs.total(i + 1, acc + xs.at(i))

nomethod fn label(prefix: str, body: str) -> str:
    prefix.concat(": ").concat(body)

scores = [95, 80, 60]
grades = loop s in scores:
    s.grade()
sum = scores.total(0, 0)
first = if grades.length() > 0:
    grades.at(0)
else:
    "none"
label("first", first)
@caption Names before use, bound values, receivers first
@value "first: top"
@quiet
@end

== WHEN THE COMPILER OBJECTS
  * Every diagnostic and warning is followed by a `help:` line naming the
    page that explains it (cli.output): read that page before rewriting.
  * A warning is reported for every call it applies to: fix them in one
    pass, and prefer deciding at the declaration (`nomethod fn`, a
    `-> List[T]` result) to rewriting call sites until the message stops
    (principle.investigate-warnings).
  * `-check FILE.bot` reports a program's diagnostics and warnings without
    running it.
  * principle.idiomatic-botlish lists the defaults that keep code
    unsurprising once it compiles.

@topic task.accumulate
title: Running totals: summing, counting, keeping a maximum
summary: Botlish has no assignment, so a running total is no name updated in a loop: pass it on as a parameter, count with a collecting loop, or keep it in a MutableArray.
index: concepts
alias: running total; accumulator; accumulate; sum a list; sum of a list; count matches; maximum of a list; loop accumulator
keywords: sum, total, count, maximum, minimum, accumulate, accumulator, running, aggregate
related: rule.immutable-bindings capability.assignment capability.map-filter concept.loops concept.mutable-array task.first-program task.iterate-with-index

== THE TRAP
    total = 0
    loop x in xs:
        total = total + x
    total
The loop body is a scope of its own, so its `total` is a new binding that
lasts for one iteration (rule.immutable-bindings). The program compiles,
and the outer `total` is still 0 after the loop.

== HOW
  * Pass the running value on: a function takes it as a parameter and
    calls itself with the next one. This is the general form (a sum, a
    maximum, a value built step by step), and the compiler proves the
    index in range.
  * Count with a collecting loop: `continue` leaves out the elements that
    do not count, and the length of the List it builds is the count.
  * When the total is state a loop updates, keep it in a mutable
    collection: a one-element MutableArray changes in place through its
    own operations (concept.mutable-array).
There is no fold or reduce function (capability.map-filter).

@example accept
import std::list

fn largest_from(xs: List[int], i: int, best: int) -> int:
    if i < 0 or i >= xs.length():
        return best
    x = xs.at(i)
    next = if x > best:
        x
    else:
        best
    xs.largest_from(i + 1, next)

[4, 9, 2].largest_from(0, 0)
@caption A maximum: the best so far passed to the next call
@value 9
@end

@example accept
import std::list

evens = loop x in [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]:
    if x.mod(2) == 1:
        continue
    x
evens.length()
@caption A count: collect what counts, then take the length
@value 3
@end

@example accept
import std::mutable_array

fn sum(xs: List[int]) -> int:
    total = std::mutable_array::create(1, 0)
    loop x in xs:
        total.set(0, total.at(0) + x)
    total.at(0)

sum([1, 2, 3])
@caption A total kept in a one-element MutableArray
@value 6
@end

@topic task.print-output
title: Printing output
summary: std::io::print_line(text) prints a line on the native backends and in executables, with std::linux::io's context installed; on every backend the program's value is printed for you.
index: stdlib backends
alias: print output; print; println; print a line; write to stdout; hello world; console output
keywords: print, output, stdout, stderr, io, hello, print_line, write
related: backend.io stdlib.std::io::print_line stdlib.module.std::io concept.context-traits concept.program-value backend.native-only task.number-text
docs: CONTEXT-TRAITS.md

== THE PROGRAM
    import std::io
    import std::linux::io

    fn hello() errors std::io::WriteFailed:
        std::io::print_line("Hello, world!")

    with context std::linux::io::create()
    hello():
        on std::io::WriteFailed:
            unit

  * `import std::io` for the portable operations (print, print_line,
    write_error, write_error_line); `import std::linux::io` for the context
    that implements them.
  * `with context std::linux::io::create()` installs it, at the entry
    program's top level, before the first call that prints
    (concept.contexts).
  * Printing can fail (std::io::WriteFailed): handle it or admit it.

@example accept
import std::io
import std::linux::io

fn hello() errors std::io::WriteFailed:
    std::io::print_line("Hello, world!")

with context std::linux::io::create()
hello():
    on std::io::WriteFailed:
        unit
@output Hello, world!
@value unit
@backends cranelift-generic cranelift
@end

== WHERE IT WORKS
Native backends (-backend cranelift or cranelift-generic) and standalone
executables, on Linux: std::linux::io makes raw system calls, which interp
and compile refuse at run time (NATIVE-ONLY; backend.io). On macOS install
std::darwin::io's context instead, on Windows std::windows::io's
(examples/io/hello-windows.bot): hello() stays the same. For output on every
backend, make the text the program's value: the compiler prints it after
"value:", and an executable prints it last.

== NUMBERS
Convert a number to text first: std::int::to_string(n) (import std::int),
std::decimal_rounded::to_string(x) for a DecimalRounded value
(task.number-text).

@topic task.handle-several-errors
title: Handling several declared errors
summary: Write one `on NAME:` handler per error a call may raise (their values may be equal); there is no `on A, B:`; a callee can instead declare one error whose payload says which failure.
index: concepts rules
alias: handle several errors; handle multiple errors; catch multiple errors; several handlers; catch multiple exceptions; handle multiple exceptions
keywords: handler, errors, several, multiple, on, payload, unhandled
related: rule.one-handler-per-error capability.multi-error-handler concept.errors concept.error-payloads rule.unhandled-errors error.unhandled-error warning.same-return-value
docs: EXPLICIT-ERROR-COMPLETIONS.md ERROR-PAYLOADS.md DIAGNOSTIC-RECOVERY.md

== ONE HANDLER PER ERROR
    moves = solve(board, 40):
        on Unsolvable:
            []
        on LimitExceeded:
            []
The compiler reports every error a call may raise that is neither handled
nor admitted, in one diagnostic ("may produce the declared errors "A", "B"
and "C""), so you see the whole set at once. A handler's value is the
call's value; `return` or `fail` in a handler leaves the function.
Handlers that end the same way are not flagged by SAME-RETURN-VALUE or
SAME-FAILURE: the language requires one handler per error.

@example accept
error Unsolvable
error LimitExceeded

fn solve(n: int) -> List[int] errors Unsolvable, LimitExceeded:
    if n < 0:
        fail Unsolvable
    if n > 40:
        fail LimitExceeded
    [n]

fn solution(n: int) -> List[int]:
    moves = solve(n):
        on Unsolvable:
            []
        on LimitExceeded:
            []
    moves

solution(50)
@value []
@quiet
@end

== ONE ERROR WITH A PAYLOAD
When every caller handles a family of failures alike, the callee's author
can declare one error that says which failure it was:
    enum Problem:
        Empty, Duplicate, OutOfRange
    error InvalidBoard:
        problem: Problem
    ...
    fail InvalidBoard {problem: Problem::Duplicate}
Callers write one handler (`on InvalidBoard {problem}:`) and can still look
at the reason.

== PASSING THEM ON
Admit some or all in the enclosing function's own `errors` clause; its
callers then decide.

@topic task.string-concatenation
title: Joining text
summary: std::str::concat(a, b) (written a.concat(b) after `import std::str`) joins two Strings, std::str::append_char(s, c) appends a character; `+` is for Ints only.
index: stdlib
alias: string concatenation; concatenate strings; join strings; append string; string plus
keywords: concat, join, append, String, text, plus
related: stdlib.std::str::concat stdlib.std::str::append_char concept.strings capability.string-formatting task.number-text stdlib.std::test::joined

== HOW
    import std::str
    greeting = "Hello, ".concat(name)
    line = greeting.append_char('!')
`+` adds Ints; applied to Strings it fails (a TYPE error). For many parts,
chain concat calls or use stdlib/test.bot's std::test::joined(parts,
separator). Numbers need std::int::to_string first (task.number-text).

@example accept
import std::str

fn greet(name: str) -> str:
    "Hello, ".concat(name).append_char('!')

greet("Botlish")
@value "Hello, Botlish!"
@quiet
@end

@topic task.iterate-with-index
title: Iterating with an index
summary: Use a lockstep loop, `loop x in xs and i from 0 to xs.length():`, to get each element and its position; the compiler proves the two clauses run equally often.
index: concepts
alias: iterate with index; enumerate; loop with index; for each with index
keywords: index, enumerate, loop, lockstep, position, element
related: concept.lockstep concept.loops proof.lockstep proof.list-bounds

== HOW
    loop x in xs and i from 0 to xs.length():
        ...x and i...
There is no enumerate function: lockstep composes any equal-length
clauses, and the compiler proves they are equal (proof.lockstep). For the
index alone, `loop i from 0 to xs.length():` with xs.at(i) is proven in
range (proof.list-bounds).

@example accept
import std::list

fn numbered(xs: List[str]) -> List[int]:
    loop x in xs and i from 0 to xs.length():
        i * 10

numbered(["one", "two", "three"])
@value [0, 10, 20]
@end

